FrameCryptor: AES-256-GCM only (strict), refuse short key material instead of
silently falling back to AES-128. Amended for finding #13: the "magic bytes"
unauthenticated-frame bypass is now gated by the Q-Audion transport policy.

Q-Audion m150 hardening tightens the m144 aes256-framecryptor.patch: that one
picked AES-256 when the shared key material was 32 bytes and otherwise quietly
kept AES-128 (password.size() == 32 ? 256 : 128). For 1:1 and group-call
E2EE we now want ONLY AES-256-GCM ever reachable through this path:
- SetKeyFromMaterial() rejects material shorter than 32 bytes outright: the
  key-ring slot is cleared (nullptr) instead of deriving a 128-bit key, so a
  short/garbled key makes encryptFrame()/decryptFrame() hit kMissingKey and
  DROP the frame (existing kMissingKey paths in the .cc) - never silently
  use a weaker cipher.
- GetAesGcmAlgorithmFromKeySize() and GetAesCbcAlgorithmFromKeySize() drop
  the `case 16` (AES-128) branch entirely, so even a caller that bypasses
  SetKeyFromMaterial() and hands a 16-byte key straight to the transformer
  gets nullptr (AesEncryptDecrypt() then returns an error) instead of a live
  AES-128 path.
- The two receive-side ratchet loops (FrameCryptorTransformer::decryptFrame
  and DataPacketCryptor::Decrypt) hardcoded DeriveKeys(..., 128). The sender
  side (RatchetKey() -> SetKeyFromMaterial()) derives 256 bits, so upstream
  receivers could never follow a ratchet, and with the case-16 AEAD removed
  that 16-byte key has no cipher at all. They now derive 256 bits (same key as
  the sender) and stop the loop instead of dereferencing a null KeySet when
  the derivation fails (upstream null-deref).
- Bounds checks on received frames (upstream memory-safety bug): decryptFrame()
  indexed data_in[size - 2], data_in[i < unencrypted_bytes] and
  data_in[size - 2 - ivLength + i] and computed size - unencrypted_bytes /
  size - ivLength - 2 without any length check, so a tiny or truncated frame
  from the peer - or, in group calls, from the SFU - read out of bounds and
  underflowed size_t into a multi-GB allocation (abort): a remote crash.
  Frames shorter than header + 12-byte IV + 2-byte trailer + 16-byte GCM tag
  are now dropped as kDecryptionFailed before any indexing, and the length is
  re-checked after RBSP unescaping. encryptFrame() gets the same guard for an
  encoder frame shorter than its clear header. Valid frames are never shorter
  than these bounds, so nothing that decrypts today is affected.
- Finding #13 (second security review): `uncrypted_magic_bytes` is a fixed,
  app-configured trailer that told decryptFrame() to treat any frame ending
  in it as "intentionally unencrypted" and deliver the rest as-is - NOT a
  decryption bypass for content we produced, but an unauthenticated-frame
  ACCEPTANCE path reachable by anyone on the media path (the remote peer, or
  in a group call the SFU), since the magic bytes are a fixed value, not a
  secret or a MAC. That let a malicious SFU or peer inject arbitrary
  "unencrypted" media/data by simply appending the known trailer. The branch
  is now gated by qaudion::MagicBytesBypassAllowed() (rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h,
  P8 - not written by this patch): hard-wired false in strict 1:1 builds, and
  true in switchable (LiveKit) builds only until the app calls the
  tighten-only qaudion::DisableMagicBytesBypass(). Both names are invented by
  this patch (not yet in the P8 sketch) and are reported to the integrator/P8
  author for reconciliation; qaudion_tuning.h itself does not ship in this
  patch (P8's own file, applied later in the series).

Compatible with LiveKit group calls: the SFU-relayed key material is always
the raw 32-byte SK_0 (Android GroupSfuKeyProvider.kt, iOS
LiveKitGroupCallRoom.swift both assert exactly 32 bytes before handing it
here), and LiveKit's internal ratchet is dormant (ratchetWindowSize=0). A
ratchet step outputs DoKeyDerivation(..., 256) = 32 bytes, so if the ratchet
is ever turned on sender and receiver stay on the same AES-256 key. Material
shorter than 32 bytes is dropped by design (fail-closed), not a bug.

Order: first in the series (m150/apply-series.sh applies P1, P2, P3, P8, P4,
P5, P6, P7). Same file as P2 (native-pli) and P3 (no-key-log); the hunks do
not overlap and `git apply --check` passes for P1 -> P2 -> P3 in that order
(re-verified after this amendment, clean-room against the exact pinned blobs;
see the crypto author's stage-1 report for the full apply log). Note: this
patch's new #include "rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h" and its qaudion:: calls are
only *declared* by P8, which is applied later in the series - by design
(P1 must stay first so P2/P3's line numbers keep matching); the tree only
becomes buildable once the full series, including P8, is applied.

Verified: `git apply --check` clean against api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.{h,cc}
at webrtc-sdk/webrtc @ ba469aa2093ba950066258ca0a59a6fbd1295582 (blobs
1b4b4def08b5 / 6d016534e2b9; the .h blob is identical to the one the m144
aes256-framecryptor.patch targeted). P2 and P3 both still apply cleanly, byte
for byte, on top of this amended P1 (checked against the exact pinned blobs
of frame_crypto_transformer.{h,cc} and p2p/base/port.cc, fetched directly
from raw.githubusercontent.com at the pinned commit - not the local partial
scan tree, which was found to differ from the pinned blobs for several files
including port.cc and pc/jsep_transport_controller.{cc,h}).

